f 1061 67159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27dfa118-efd1-5317-966f-90a083509c55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069916 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069916 #edb/ref 1061 67160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ebe74c08-cc12-564f-b8bf-ce093355d7a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069917 #edb/ref 1061 67164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c918cb7-6f6b-5686-99e8-54b94b6009e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 #edb/ref 1064 "The **$y$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $y$-axis. For instance, consider the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and has a slope of $3.$ How can we find the $y$-intercept of this line? \n\n![](images/c3/c307a60266b77cb497a5c5b02a6d9518a3b31e22b1ec97afb1d2b40f488982cb.png)\n\nWe already know values for $x$, $y$ and $m,$ so we can just substitute them into the equation $y=mx+b$ and then solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =mx+b \\\\ (-1) & =(3)(1)+b \\\\ -1 & =3+b \\\\ b & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{blue}b}=-4.$ The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 #edb/ref 1065 8059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f9c0e73-8a80-5472-bf82-4c78c873c181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 #edb/ref 1064 "The **$x$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $x$-axis. For instance, consider the line $y = 2x - 4$. How can we find the $x$-intercept of this line?\n\n![](images/57/57a31eab932c948358e7ab6e428707a6a4b9f551bc956554c3a16b659b5f7988.png)\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-4 \\\\ (0) & =2x-4 \\\\ 4 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$**-intercept** is $x=2.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 #edb/ref 1065 8060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fbd0b44d-8460-5058-9b94-c21852d09a4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the X-Intercept of a Straight Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1039 "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of Two Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073724 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1a94905c-48fe-5662-bb6c-2153f7725648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073725 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073725 #edb/ref 1039 "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of a Fraction and an Integer" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073725 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073725 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ea092786-a281-534e-803b-90e6c23b90a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1039 "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of Two Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073726 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d34e60c9-710b-50ca-a491-adc2b50e6edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Problems Using Unit Rates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073976 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a0efe329-d527-57d7-9005-092c45b3c758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074616 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074616 #edb/ref 1061 17329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074616 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18311dd6-9a0a-534e-9a8d-3794869d485b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074616 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074617 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074617 #edb/ref 1061 17330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074617 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b3df2fe0-94ac-5bcc-a3b1-52b4d1b4236b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074617 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074618 #edb/ref 1061 17331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074618 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "347b63ef-9c0b-5688-8e27-d7df776461a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074618 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074629 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074629 #edb/ref 1061 18413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7987d98c-69c0-57a6-b100-cd3d9e53820b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075005 #edb/ref 1064 "Unit rates can be used to solve many real-world problems in which we are given two quantities and wish to find what happens to one quantity when we vary the other.\n\nFor example, we might know the total cost of a specific number of items and be asked to find the cost for a different number of the same item.\n\nTo solve a problem using unit rates, the first step is to compute the unit rate by dividing the two given quantities. Then, we can use the unit rate to calculate the desired quantity.\n\nLet's see some examples!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075005 #edb/ref 1065 3313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075005 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fc623ecb-782c-5182-90e7-e8b6720b2780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075005 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186077655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186077656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186077657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186077658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186077659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186077657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077654 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "41d9b076-e24e-5725-bd8f-2293f79ac91a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077655 #edb/ref 1080 "Not enough information" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077655 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077655 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9cad4dd8-ca6d-55ab-9dc5-081de80e69a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077656 #edb/ref 1080 "No, since there is at least one output with more than one input" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077656 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077656 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "001094b5-522b-5aff-87d8-cdfd551294ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077657 #edb/ref 1080 "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077657 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077657 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3024a1e1-3c8f-5f6d-ab79-bde1c13d5709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077658 #edb/ref 1080 "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077658 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077658 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4cd5fdb7-c2a9-539d-b5b1-60da0e4d051a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077659 #edb/ref 1080 "No, since both sets have different numbers of elements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077659 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186077659 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6008c3f2-d2aa-5fd1-96b6-5ae4ce87bd88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining if a Rule Given by a Mapping Diagram Is a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078527 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3cc2167e-d318-54d5-8b67-47afbc65f530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078528 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078528 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078528 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1045 "Visual Representations of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078655 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fa63b04f-e0ce-592f-b214-495b5c1b67a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079450 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079450 #edb/ref 1061 3581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079450 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93acbf1d-7504-5269-982c-7785e36bfa3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079450 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079451 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079451 #edb/ref 1061 3585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079451 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "953349df-46b8-543b-a772-87c3e0ffd977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079451 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079452 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079452 #edb/ref 1061 36150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079452 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4034d183-7b91-5f03-a1ca-b71ce2a3ea8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079452 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079453 #edb/ref 1061 37242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079453 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d65897aa-199d-5bb6-a05a-f5877fe63002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079453 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079454 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079454 #edb/ref 1061 37341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079454 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53c16c15-06ad-566b-bb58-7baf803dbf46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079454 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079455 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079455 #edb/ref 1061 37364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079455 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2bd4f1a-fe61-532e-a324-4c924a280b8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079455 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079616 #edb/ref 1064 "A **function** is a rule where *every* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the rule defined by\n\n$$\nf(x) = x+3\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ can be any number from the **set** $\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$ A set is a collection of numbers we can use in our rule. \n\nLet's calculate the possible values of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =1+3=4 \\\\ f(2) & =2+3=5 \\\\ f(3) & =3+3=6 \\\\ f(4) & =4+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this rule using a **mapping diagram**, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4714001592222932934cb7edd5852542a2e990e6680daad85e2db88b9c7cef47.png)\n\nThis rule *is* a function because *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079616 #edb/ref 1065 1488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079616 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "88ebc343-41ab-500a-8668-451bbf36ec8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079616 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079694 #edb/ref 1064 "Now let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/b0/b02a511f8043bb7e39df2de082befa267f795948ea6c1cc1ef38eb9df4a8cb1d.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is assigned to *more than one* output. In this case, the input $x=1$ is mapped to *both* $2$ and $-2.$\n\nFinally, let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/be/bea4ee8835a6111e105670863edede9450ad26dbfbea631454d5c8d6cd76a6a1.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is not assigned to an output. In this case, the input $0$ is not assigned an output." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079694 #edb/ref 1065 5913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079694 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f58c4e80-86e3-5b65-99a3-2a16208231e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079694 #edb/ref 1067 "Examples of Rules That Are Not Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079695 #edb/ref 1064 "When a rule is represented as a table, we have a function if and only if all values in the *first row* are *distinct* (i.e., different).\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\nThe rule defined by the following table is a function. Notice that all values in the first row are distinct. Every input value is assigned to exactly one output value.\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, the rule defined by the following table is *not* a function. Notice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}3$ twice. The input value $3$ is assigned to two output values ($0$ and $-1$).\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $4$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $5$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079695 #edb/ref 1065 6156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079695 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f5d2e3d4-e985-565a-81f8-c8b8e93dc73d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079695 #edb/ref 1067 "Rules Represented Using Tables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079696 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we must remember that a rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079696 #edb/ref 1065 6161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079696 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f92a951f-7b57-51b4-b130-bf0d4c0da4a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079696 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1039 "Rational Equations With Variables in One Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083192 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "783c067b-9676-57f1-81a9-7724c18e1534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083193 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083193 #edb/ref 1039 "Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083193 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083193 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "20c12caf-9f3e-5dcb-a12c-5c368824ede9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083194 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083194 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083194 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083194 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083194 #edb/ref 1039 "Rational Equations With Binomials in One Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083194 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186082938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083194 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a8f96a06-1ebb-5ed1-9e47-23525e2a756e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Rational Equations Using Cross-Multiplication" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083407 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "654c7bcb-8774-5d52-8516-2b544db9deef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084066 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084066 #edb/ref 1061 170984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084066 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc5abf0d-f453-5c16-a078-3ade2b5464f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084066 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084067 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084067 #edb/ref 1061 170985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084067 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a296ae16-d9ac-5336-88fb-84c518347b2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084067 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084068 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084068 #edb/ref 1061 170988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084068 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ccd40655-e59d-59b1-925d-4907d84cca52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084068 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084069 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084069 #edb/ref 1061 170989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084069 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cdcec66-242d-5007-990f-d0802020baee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084069 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084070 #edb/ref 1061 170992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084070 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1970d372-6794-5a8a-8180-36e7434e572b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084070 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084516 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x}=\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhen the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nBefore we start, though, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because when $x=0$, we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side.\n\nNow, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =x\\cdot 3 \\\\ 10 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can solve for $x$ using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x= \\dfrac{10}{3}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x = \\dfrac{10}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084516 #edb/ref 1065 8779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084516 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "89ea4830-364e-5105-8df0-54cf9f7e1e36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084516 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084517 #edb/ref 1064 "If there is a negative sign in a rational equation, we can still solve it using cross-multiplication. The only difference is that we have to move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction first.\n\nFor example, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = -\\dfrac 9 8 .\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the equation from the previous example, but this time, there is a negative sign on the right-hand side.\n\nWe deal with the negative sign by moving it onto the numerator of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed to solve the equation by cross-multiplication, as usual. (We also note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.) Cross-multiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} \\[5pt]\n\t\t3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot (-9) \\[5pt]\n\t\t24 &= -18x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t24 &= -18x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{24}{-18} &= \\dfrac{-18x}{-18} \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = -\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084517 #edb/ref 1065 8780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084517 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d61fcf8b-f639-5c4c-8997-66813230bedf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084517 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating an Exponential Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096657 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9283940a-cad8-51b5-b8c2-dc42ad8de264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096658 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096957 #edb/