-905e612555d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1061 623186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2896eab9-aca7-5163-8e08-2101c4fa2827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1061 623189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffc5ce2a-4bcb-5660-a1ce-e79bd1b4c604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1061 623190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec3bbafa-69db-5676-8013-221157e979a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106814 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106814 #edb/ref 1061 3541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106814 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "139aab34-d09f-505c-96f5-208298444874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106814 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106815 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106815 #edb/ref 1061 3542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106815 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7305de37-518a-5a4e-903e-40552a4f9962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106815 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106816 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106816 #edb/ref 1061 3545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106816 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "196b5dd3-ef4e-520a-aadd-9e763e63db72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106816 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106817 #edb/ref 1061 3548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106817 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5b6259b-d42d-5bce-bbd7-530a36ded592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106817 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107037 #edb/ref 1064 "The **power of product rule** states that if we want to raise the product $xy$ to some power $n$, we must raise each factor to the power $n$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n(xy)^n=x^n y^n\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n(xy)^4=x^4y^4.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way, changing the order of factors in the multiplication. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(xy)^{4} & = \\\\ (xy)(xy)(xy)(xy) & = \\\\ (x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x)(y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107037 #edb/ref 1065 1482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107037 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7aabbc0f-a3ca-5f05-b6ae-d7551b16ae1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107037 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46a85010d46969ffb7df440efd72bb3fa8d1394b35cc2887ee4567cda15079c5.png)\n\nThe sum of the length of its sides is called the **perimeter**. Here, the perimeter of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\  & =2+4.2+3.8 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1065 2244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7664d7c3-e47c-57b6-97aa-1131f036852c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that ${AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m},$ and $CD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}.$ What is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?   \n\n![](images/a7/a74251f1c72eccad42b98e0d3fd9a7e7cd0a60018a6f03bb65ec2abc34eb1c88.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AD+AB+BC+CD \\\\  & =6+2+4+5 \\\\  & =17\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1065 2246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0428807f-e6a6-5280-81bc-ab454203aebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1067 "Perimeters of Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the polygons depicted below and find their perimeters.\n\n![](images/69/69ef06a71f13625ad4525a03966f4a4c62ae2b2136b085e97b8dc02a6a6963cc.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we conclude that polygons I, II and III all have a perimeter of $10$ while polygon IV has a perimeter of $8.$ \n\nPolygons that have the same perimeter are called **isoperimetric**. So I, II and III are isoperimetric polygons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1065 2247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "596db00e-9cf7-51e1-902e-73c6670780eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1067 "Isoperimetric Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110005 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f75d50b2-6f5a-5bce-9e0b-522b9985c2e5" #ed